    "title": "Got a snow squall alert? Here’s what it means",
    "text": "Snow squalls are short, intense bursts of snow that can turn roads dangerous in minutes. In the midst of the latest wintry weather, you may have gotten an alert about a snow squall. If you’re wondering what that is, and how it differs from a snowstorm, here’s what to know. What is a snow squall? According to the National Weather Service, a snow squall is a brief but intense burst of snowfall that can cause sudden whiteout conditions, rapidly falling temperatures and icy roads within minutes. Snow squalls are often associated with strong cold fronts and typically last less than an hour. Unlike major winter storms, snow squalls can occur without a larger system in place and may only produce minor snow accumulation of an inch or less. But experts warn that the danger comes from how quickly conditions worsen, particularly for drivers. Visibility can drop to near zero, and road surfaces can freeze almost instantly, creating hazardous travel conditions. Snow squall vs snowstorm The main difference between a snow squall and a snowstorm is duration. Snow squalls are short-lived but extremely intense, while snowstorms can last for hours or even days and generally affect a broader area. The National Weather Service issues snow squall warnings when conditions pose an immediate threat, especially to motorists. Officials urge drivers to avoid or delay travel when a warning is issued, noting there is no safe place on the highway during a snow squall. If drivers are already on the road, NOAA recommends slowing down, turning on headlights and hazard lights, allowing extra distance between vehicles and avoiding sudden braking, which can lead to loss of control. The National Weather Service advises travelers to check forecasts and alerts at weather.gov before heading out during winter weather.",
